---
layout: null
---
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
{% comment %}
  ===========================================================================
  ONE feed for posts AND projects.
  ===========================================================================

  This file overrides jekyll-feed. The plugin's generator does
  `next if file_exists?(path)`, so the moment a source `feed.xml` exists the
  plugin stops generating one and this template wins. The plugin stays in
  `_config.yml` on purpose: `{% raw %}{% feed_meta %}{% endraw %}` in the layout is a separate tag
  and still emits the autodiscovery <link>.

  Why override rather than add a second feed: jekyll-feed CAN publish extra
  feeds per collection, but only at `/feed/<name>.xml` — a second address to
  advertise, subscribe to and keep alive. Projects are infrequent enough that
  splitting them off would mostly produce a feed nobody subscribes to.

  WHAT MUST NOT CHANGE: the entry <id>. It is the key a reader uses to decide
  whether it has seen an item before, so altering the scheme re-notifies every
  subscriber about every old post. Posts keep jekyll-feed's exact scheme,
  `post.id | absolute_url`, which is the URL WITHOUT the .html extension and is
  not the same string as the <link href>. Verified byte-identical against the
  pre-override feed. Projects key on their own absolute URL.

  Merging two different types in Liquid, which has no way to sort across them:
  build one array of sortable strings, `DATE|kind|RANK|INDEX`, sort THAT, then
  use INDEX to reach back into the right source array. RANK is `999 - index` so
  that the reverse sort leaves same-date items in their authored order rather
  than flipping them; it stays three digits up to 899 projects.
{% endcomment %}
<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom">
  <generator uri="https://jekyllrb.com/" version="{{ jekyll.version }}">Jekyll</generator>
  <link href="{{ '/feed.xml' | absolute_url }}" rel="self" type="application/atom+xml" />
  <link href="{{ '/' | absolute_url }}" rel="alternate" type="text/html" />
  <updated>{{ site.time | date_to_xmlschema }}</updated>
  <id>{{ '/feed.xml' | absolute_url | xml_escape }}</id>
  <title type="html">{{ site.title | smartify | xml_escape }}</title>
  <subtitle>{{ site.description | xml_escape }}</subtitle>
  {% comment %}
    Feed-level author. jekyll-feed emitted `<author><name></name></author>` on
    every entry because `site.author` is unset, and an empty name is not valid
    Atom — a feed needs an author somewhere. Declaring it once here covers
    every entry by inheritance and drops the empty per-entry elements.
  {% endcomment %}
  <author><name>{{ site.title | xml_escape }}</name></author>

  {% comment %}
    Same rule as /posts/ and as jekyll-feed before it: `draft: true` withholds a
    post from the feed. "Draft" means exactly one thing site-wide.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% assign published = site.posts | where_exp: "post", "post.draft != true" | sort: "date" | reverse %}

  {% assign rows = "" | split: "" %}
  {% for post in published %}
    {% capture row %}{{ post.date | date: "%Y%m%d%H%M%S" }}|p|{{ 999 | minus: forloop.index0 }}|{{ forloop.index0 }}{% endcapture %}
    {% assign one = row | split: "~" %}
    {% assign rows = rows | concat: one %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% for pr in site.data.projects %}
    {% capture row %}{{ pr.date | date: "%Y%m%d%H%M%S" }}|r|{{ 999 | minus: forloop.index0 }}|{{ forloop.index0 }}{% endcapture %}
    {% assign one = row | split: "~" %}
    {% assign rows = rows | concat: one %}
  {% endfor %}
  {% assign rows = rows | sort | reverse %}

  {% comment %}
    20, not jekyll-feed's 10: the limit now has to cover two streams, and at 10
    a burst of posts would silently push every project out of the feed.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% for row in rows limit: 20 %}
  {% assign parts = row | split: "|" %}
  {% assign idx = parts[3] | plus: 0 %}

  {% if parts[1] == "p" %}
  {% assign post = published[idx] %}
  {% assign post_title = post.title | smartify | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | xml_escape %}
    <entry>
      <title type="html">{{ post_title }}</title>
      <link href="{{ post.url | absolute_url }}" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="{{ post_title }}" />
      <published>{{ post.date | date_to_xmlschema }}</published>
      <updated>{{ post.last_modified_at | default: post.date | date_to_xmlschema }}</updated>
      <id>{{ post.id | absolute_url | xml_escape }}</id>
      <content type="html" xml:base="{{ post.url | absolute_url | xml_escape }}"><![CDATA[{{ post.content | strip }}]]></content>
      {% for category in post.categories %}<category term="{{ category | xml_escape }}" />
      {% endfor %}<category term="post" />
      {% assign post_summary = post.description | default: post.excerpt %}
      {% if post_summary and post_summary != empty %}
      <summary type="html"><![CDATA[{{ post_summary | strip_html | normalize_whitespace }}]]></summary>
      {% endif %}
    </entry>

  {% else %}
  {% assign pr = site.data.projects[idx] %}
  {% comment %}
    An entry's URL may already be absolute — the Medicaid catalogue lives in its
    own repository — and `absolute_url` would happily prefix the site root onto
    it and produce a dead link. Test before converting.
  {% endcomment %}
  {% if pr.url contains "://" %}{% assign pr_url = pr.url %}{% else %}{% assign pr_url = pr.url | absolute_url %}{% endif %}
  {% assign pr_title = pr.title | smartify | strip_html | normalize_whitespace | xml_escape %}
    <entry>
      <title type="html">{{ pr_title }}</title>
      <link href="{{ pr_url | xml_escape }}" rel="alternate" type="text/html" title="{{ pr_title }}" />
      <published>{{ pr.date | date_to_xmlschema }}</published>
      <updated>{{ pr.date | date_to_xmlschema }}</updated>
      <id>{{ pr_url | xml_escape }}</id>
      {% comment %}
        Projects carry no inline content: they are standalone pages with their
        own scripts and stylesheets, and pasting one into a reader would give a
        broken copy rather than the thing itself. The blurb plus a link is the
        honest payload. Both <content> and <summary> are set, because readers
        differ in which one they show and neither is guaranteed.
      {% endcomment %}
      <content type="html"><![CDATA[<p>{{ pr.blurb | strip_html | normalize_whitespace }}</p><p><a href="{{ pr_url }}">Open the project</a></p>]]></content>
      <category term="project" />
      <summary type="html"><![CDATA[{{ pr.blurb | strip_html | normalize_whitespace }}]]></summary>
    </entry>
  {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
</feed>
